    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e63d2e9e069d3d95443934b02f5c525bc1131dfd63b92d5eda19f2d9e2884e07",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A putative zero produces a reciprocal-distance term in ζ′/ζ, while Euler-product positivity bounds a nonnegative combination of logarithmic derivatives at selected heights. Nonnegative trigonometric polynomials parameterize that combination and reduce one part of the exclusion problem to a scalar objective; spectral-factor autocorrelations preserve polynomial nonnegativity during search.",
