    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e70d763f287d2b395106ccd6f5123959199f64d78605e653dbdbc2dbe07e7361",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Truncating a zero-driven sine series makes simultaneous phase alignment finite-dimensional; a tail bound and Dirichlet recurrence then transfer a large transform value to arbitrarily distant arithmetic oscillations. Because the eventual gain can grow only like an iterated logarithm, rigorous sign reversal is compatible with an apparently stable sign over enormous computed ranges.",
